      **Collection criterion — AST shape, identifier-insensitive.**

      Walk every `src/**/*.ts` file (including tests). Do not pass a
      hardcoded file list. Parse with `ts.createSourceFile`. Collect
      every function-like (FunctionDeclaration, FunctionExpression,
      ArrowFunction, MethodDeclaration — including `export` / `const`
      bindings) whose body is a single `return` of an object literal
      whose properties, **order-independent**, are exactly:

        tag        ← <p>.tag
        attributes ← { ...<p>.attributes }     (spread of <p>.attributes)
        children   ← <p>.children.map(<self>)  (or .map(x => <self>(x)))
        text       ← <p>.text

      where <p> is the function's sole parameter and <self> is the
      function's own binder (declaration name or const name). Anonymous
      arrows have no <self> and do not match — so the inline objects in
      grace-cursor.ts that `.map(cloneXmlNode)` (call the helper, not
      themselves) are not hits, and neither is appendEpoch's one-level
      `[...child.children]` arrow.

      That is the "normalized function body" the spec asked for, stated
      as a shape rather than as a string hash: binders are holes, trivia
      and type annotations are ignored, property order is ignored. A
      copy named `duplicateNode` (or anything else) with this body is a
      hit. The live suite assertion is:

        hits.length === 1
        hits[0].file ends with src/artifact/xml.ts
        hits[0].exported === true

